                "overview": {
                    "text": "Trendy restaurant offering an upscale plant-based menu inspired by Asian flavors, plus cocktails.",
                    "languageCode": "en-US"
                },
                "description": {
                    "text": "Upscale vegan restaurant in Downtown's Dupont Circle neighborhood featuring Asian-inspired cuisine.\nThe plant-based menu includes sushi, dumplings, and noodles. People recommend the bang bang broccoli, dan dan noodles, and bubble waffle. There's a happy hour, and the drinks menu features cocktails.\nThe trendy venue has a bar, late-night vibes, and a romantic, cozy atmosphere. Some people praise the ambiance and service, while others say the service is slow and unfriendly. Reservations are recommended.\nCustomers typically spend $30–50.",
                    "languageCode": "en-US"
                },
                "references": {
                    "reviews": [
                        {
                            "name": "places/ChIJ7aCfw2C3t4kRvb6eK8AmDGQ/reviews/ChZDSUhNMG9nS0VJQ0FnSUNKN19PME13EAE",
                            "relativePublishTimeDescription": "a year ago",
                            "rating": 3,
                            "text": {
                                "text": "The new location is nice. Service was good. The food is just meh— pretty average to be honest.\n\n- Bang bang was a bit cold. 5/10\n- 1000 layer potato was good— nothing life changing. 6/10\n- Pad Thai Slaw was pretty good, it had a nice crunch and much better than the Bethesda Location 8/10\n- Crispy Gyoza was crispy, filling was food but it needed a chili oil. 6/10\n- Bubble Waffle Ice Cream was the best thing we ate! The taro ice cream was perfection. Highly recommend 9/10\n\nIt’s unfortunate that restaurants in DC aren’t more accommodating to substitutions and add-ons. My husband wanted to order the lettuce wraps. However, he does not eat tofu, and when he asked if he could substitute it for more mushrooms, he was told he could not. Soy is something that many people are allergic to, and it would make sense that there would be substitutions for soy in dishes.",
                                "languageCode": "en"
                            },
                            "originalText": {
                                "text": "The new location is nice. Service was good. The food is just meh— pretty average to be honest.\n\n- Bang bang was a bit cold. 5/10\n- 1000 layer potato was good— nothing life changing. 6/10\n- Pad Thai Slaw was pretty good, it had a nice crunch and much better than the Bethesda Location 8/10\n- Crispy Gyoza was crispy, filling was food but it needed a chili oil. 6/10\n- Bubble Waffle Ice Cream was the best thing we ate! The taro ice cream was perfection. Highly recommend 9/10\n\nIt’s unfortunate that restaurants in DC aren’t more accommodating to substitutions and add-ons. My husband wanted to order the lettuce wraps. However, he does not eat tofu, and when he asked if he could substitute it for more mushrooms, he was told he could not. Soy is something that many people are allergic to, and it would make sense that there would be substitutions for soy in dishes.",
                                "languageCode": "en"
                            },
                            "authorAttribution": {
                                "displayName": "Raisa Crespo",
                                "uri": "https://www.google.com/maps/contrib/100527420159757856478/reviews",
                                "photoUri": "https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjUWuEX672zPAZ0cCHlYXXR1NnXqX8LRqa_rqWGLMdgCYXB7H-w=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo"
